Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 15:32:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 024/106] ASoC: tas2562: mark TDM_CFG2 volatile so the channel
 mux stops self-clobbering

TDM_CFG2 (0x08) packs two unrelated fields: the ASI1 channel-select mux
in bits 5:4 (a DAPM mux) and the I2S RX word/slot length in bits 3:2 and
1:0 (written from hw_params via set_bitwidth). Two subsystems, two trigger
times, one register, both doing read-modify-write through the regmap
cache.

Around a stream start they interleave, and with the cached copy stale one
RMW stamps an old value back over the other's bits. In practice the
channel select loses: bits 5:4 land on 0, which is the mux's I2C-offset /
mute code, so the amp goes silent. Observed on a Xiaomi surya driving two
of these amps as a stereo pair - setting each amp to its own channel (top
= Left, bottom = Right) via UCM at profile-activation time intermittently
muted whichever amp, not value-specific, while setting the same mux during
steady playback (after hw_params has run) was always reliable.

Mark the register volatile so every RMW reads the live value first; then
neither the mux write nor set_bitwidth can clobber the other's bits, and
true per-channel stereo becomes reliable.
---
 sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c
index 81f6caff2..e95fce883 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2562.c
@@ -943,9 +943,26 @@ static const struct regmap_range_cfg tas2562_ranges[] = {
 	},
 };
 
+/*
+ * TDM_CFG2 packs two unrelated fields written by two different subsystems at
+ * two different times: the channel-select mux (bits 5:4, a DAPM mux) and the
+ * I2S word/slot length (bits 3:2 and 1:0, written from hw_params). Both do
+ * read-modify-write. If the cached copy is stale when they interleave - which
+ * it is around a stream start - one stamps a stale value back over the other's
+ * bits, and the channel select lands on the mute code (bits 5:4 = 0). Making
+ * the register volatile forces every RMW to read the live value first, so
+ * neither writer can clobber the other. This is what makes true L/R stereo
+ * (each amp on its own channel) reliable instead of intermittently muting.
+ */
+static bool tas2562_volatile_reg(struct device *dev, unsigned int reg)
+{
+	return reg == TAS2562_TDM_CFG2;
+}
+
 static const struct regmap_config tas2562_regmap_config = {
 	.reg_bits = 8,
 	.val_bits = 8,
+	.volatile_reg = tas2562_volatile_reg,
 
 	.max_register = 254 * 128,
 	/*
